{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1542189822,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzODYwNjY3OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2996#issuecomment-438606678"},"message":"I think is worthwhile to equip run with this ability. It would become much more practical to use `run` inside datalad commands.\n\nFew aspects that I think are worth keeping in mind when we are moving in this direction.\n\n1. locking or blocking until unlocked: eventually we will want to be able to use datalad in a multi-process fashion. It would make sense to make `run` aware of other `run` calls happening at the same time and allow it to wait from them to finish.\n2. non-cmdline \"runs\": with the growing infrastructure in place within `run`, it is worth thinking about the possibility for non-cmdline runners, ie. Python code (like datalad api calls that run). if we would be able to push regular api calls through run, we might be able to achieve process-level parallelization in a few select places, without breaking a sweat.\n\nOn the specific topic here:\n\nI am torn. Adding a line prefix will prevent interoperation with in general-purpose JSON processors that could have a stream of JSON records pipe into them. OTOH: just producing JSON records would force `run` to test convert any output line to see if it could be a datalad result. Maybe that could be combined with a cheap test if a line is a JSON record to speed things up.\n\nMy gut seems to suggest that going with `-f json` in combination with a minimum set of criteria that need to be fulfilled to consider a particular JSON record to be a datalad result (that needs to be yielded) could be a viable and still robust approach.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1542192261,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzODYxODgwMQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2996#issuecomment-438618801"},"message":"\u003e My gut seems to suggest that going with -f json in combination with a minimum set of criteria that need to be fulfilled to consider a particular JSON record to be a datalad result (that needs to be yielded) could be a viable and still robust approach.\n\nThink so, too. WRT to other JSON processors we could have a marker saying \"the next X lines are datalad results\", leaving the actual lines untouched so they can still be fed into something else, while simultanously `run` can have a comparably cheap test before firing up a JSON parser. Plus: streaming the entire thing into something else that expects a plain JSON stream would still need an in between filter to ignore those marker lines, but this one is simple and could come with datalad as a shell script for example. But note, that this is only relevant if you really need to have it both ways. If it is a dedicated result renderer that is marking those results, you can still use `-f json` as before in a lot of cases.\n\nre 1:\nNot sure how to deal with that. Think of a run call to something that calls run itself. Do we want to prohibit that kind of procedures? Or can you think of a way to detect this situation?\n\nre 2:\nAgree. Worth mentioning that we do have this concept for `Runner` as well although it's hardly used. Intention there was to be able to apply \"protocols\" to callables and syscalls.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1542211535,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzODcxNjkyMw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2996#issuecomment-438716923"},"message":"\u003e However, it seems worth discussing it in a more general fashion and may be come up with a solution in `datalad-run` itself.\n\nYeah, I think it makes sense to have a general solution for this.\n\n\u003e What else seems to be a thinkable approach to you guys?\n\nI can't think of anything besides the approaches you've already outline.\n\nI too lean towards parsing stdout, and I agree with Michael's gut in terms of starting with `-f json` without markers, optimizing from there as needed.  I'm not sure it's worth the complication, but I also wonder whether we should add a parameter to `run_command` and perhaps `Run.__call__` that controls whether we even consider parsing.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1542212404,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzODcyMjQ1MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2996#issuecomment-438722450"},"message":"I also wonder about another aspect of it. If `run` harvests such a result from stdout, should it then swallow it? Assuming `run` would capture stdout, possibly parse it and then spitting it out again, so actual user output is still going to stdout (from perspective of the `run` caller). If it's meant to be yielded as a result by `run` it doesn't need to go to final output (kind of doubling it), right?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1542212548,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDQzODcyMzE0MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2996#issuecomment-438723140"},"message":"\u003e  If it's meant to be yielded as a result by `run` it doesn't need to go to final output (kind of doubling it), right?\n\nThat was my thinking, yes.","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1542212548,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDE1MTYzNDUxMg=="},"target":"93d29957e42c417dcb4cc413e96bc87802f182c92441029edcd41851cb4a941b","message":"\u003e  If it's meant to be yielded as a result by `run` it doesn't need to go to final output (kind of doubling it), right?\n\nThat was my thinking too.","files":null}]}